  "Source": "G. Hass, \"Preparation, properties and optical applications of thin films of titanium dioxide,\" Vacuum 2(4), 331-345 (1952). (doi not printed in the PDF) - Fig. 8 caption p. 338 and text p. 339 ('The first combination consists of a film of magnesium fluoride having a thickness of half a wavelength at lambda = 450 millimicrons, and a film of titanium dioxide one quarter-wavelength thick at lambda = 530 millimicrons')",
  "Notes": "Achromatic two-layer beam divider: half-wave MgF2 (at 450 nm) under a quarter-wave rutile film (at 530 nm)\nSpecification: Reflectance substantially achromatic (> 44 %) throughout the visible without appreciable loss.\nReported: Text p. 339: R more than 44 % without appreciable loss over a rather extended spectral region. Fig. 8 (plot only, approximate): R about 44-46 % and T about 53-54 % nearly flat from 400 to 800 nm.\nNotes: MgF2 half-wave at 450 nm = optical thickness 225 nm = 225/132.5 = 1.698113 QWOT at the 530 nm reference (physical 163.0 nm with n = 1.38). MgF2 is assumed next to the glass (it is named first, and the half-wave achromatising layer of Turner's scheme lies under the QW high-index film; TiO2 is described as the top film). Constant rutile n = 2.713 ignores the dispersion of Fig. 5, so the achromatism will differ somewhat.\nLayers from Fig. 8 caption p. 338 and text p. 339 ('The first combination consists of a film of magnesium fluoride having a thickness of half a wavelength at lambda = 450 millimicrons, and a film of titanium dioxide one quarter-wavelength thick at lambda = 530 millimicrons'); layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours R >= 39.5 % from 400 to 800 nm; paper R > 44 % across the visible. Ours dips to 39.5 % at the ends of the range, against the paper’s R > 44 %; the rutile index here is constant (2.713), while real TiO2 is dispersive.",
